\begin{abstract}
  \whyse (WEB Hypertext System in Emacs) is an integrated development
  environment for noweb-based hypertexts within Emacs. While named for
  WEB, it does not support that nor CWEB; the name is primarily to
  reference the inspiration. It is motivated by, and based on, a
  conference proceeding written in 1991 by Brown and
  Czejdo.
\end{abstract}





\part{\whyse{} and its implementation}
\label{part:what-is-whyse}





\chapter{Preface}\label{Preface}
% I began this project some time in a month of May before I began a course
% in databases and query languages, as well as systems analysis. I wanted
% to get a step ahead. I found, years later, I have not.

The RDBMS architecture proposed by Brown \& Czejdo isn't implemented in
this version of \whyse{}, but for completeness is planned. The main
features implemented are indices for chunks and identifiers, in the
style of the \reftex{} table of contents as well as other views, a graph
view of chunks and identifiers, and a VC-based project-oriented workflow
used as the backend of these features (the function \texttt{vc-root-dir}
and some features of customize are relied upon).

Users of \whyse{} are expected to be familiar with noweb. Users must
know how to write their own pipelines rather than only using
\texttt{noweave} and \texttt{notangle} (the noweb manual explaining the
\texttt{-v} option is a starting point for those not familiar enough
with noweb). Knowing how to write filters isn't required.

To use either noweb or \whyse{} to their fullest, users should read and
understand the \textit{noweb Hacker's Guide}. Reading the implementation
of \whyse{} afterwards is recommended; reading the implementation of
peg-noweb isn't required unless you're a serious hacker.

Modifying \whyse{} without using \whyse{} would be counter-intuitive,
yet effort is made to produce readable output code and structured,
readable webs. Nevertheless, unless something is broken you should try
to leverage \whyse{} to improve itself.

The implementation of the whyse \textit{package} begins with the indices
feature.





\chapter{Indices}
\label{cha:indices}
\whs described several indices used for navigation between modules.
These indices are the core idea implemented in \whyse, whereas the
\sql architecture and windowing are deliberately avoided. The indices
specified in \whs were,
\begin{itemize}
\item identifier index
\item topical index
\item module index
\item section index
\end{itemize}
whereas I only plan to implement the identifier and module indices in
the first release of \whyse. The topical index depends upon specific
behaviour of the traditional WEB. The section index is already suitably,
and better-implemented, by RefTeX. Eventually, extension of RefTeX and
AUCTeX is the goal, rather than providing a wholly different interface.

\section{Module index}
% TODO: whyse--make-index-modules
The module index provides a tabular or graphical interface to the
references to other modules made by each module.

TODO: The tabular interface is provided by the \texttt{ctable} package,
which requires tabular data be provided in a list of rows, so the parse
tree of a noweb document is first gathered.

Similarly, the graphical interface provided is provided by the
\texttt{dag-draw} package, which requires an adjacency matrix in list
form.

The \latex command \texttt{\nowebchunks} emits a formatted chunk index
using data accumulated throughout the compilation of a latex document.
The accumulated data is based off of the labels and cross-references
inserted into the document by the \texttt{-index} argument of
\textit{noweave}, which adds the \textit{noidx} and \textit{finduses}
filters to the pipeline. E.g. \texttt{noweave -index whyse.nw}.

WHYSE uses the noweb programs installed on the host, and requires users
to reconstruct the noweb's compilation pipeline; this is the simplest
approach. The pipeline (below) that compiles \whyse itself shows the
stages noweb goes through to produce a tool syntax containing everything
that \latex{} needs to construct the hyperlinks from the indices back to
definitions or usages.
<<the noweave pipeline with autodefs and indexing used to compile \whyse{}'s literate documentation>>=
markup whyse.nw | ./autodefs.elisp | finduses | noidx -delay
@ 
The information necessary to create a hyperlink back to the source text from
the index is already availble in the tool syntax, so all that needs to be done is to
transform it and add metadata usable by ctable hook functions or other
Emacs SVG-related packages.

<<the [[whyse-open-project-functions]] abnormal hook and its default functions>>=
(defun whyse--make-index-modules ();whyse--make-index-identifiers is counterpart
  "Create the *whyse-modules* buffer and show it in a window."
  (let ((modbuf (get-buffer-create "*whyse-modules*"))
        g g-nodes g-edges)
    (with-current-buffer modbuf
      <<create the dag svg and insert it>>
      (split-window)
      (switch-to-buffer-other-window modbuf))))
@ 

Following the tutorials provided by \textit{dag-draw}, I will [[@<<create
the dag svg and insert it>>]] into the current buffer.
<<create the dag svg and insert it>>=
<<set [[g-nodes]] and [[g-edges]]>>
(setq g (dag-draw-create-from-spec :nodes g-nodes :edges g-edges))
(dag-draw-layout-graph g)
(princ (dag-draw-render-graph g 'ascii))
@
The nodes and edges need to be set by translating a small AWK program
to elisp, a program that emits adjacency matrix, very nearly.
<<hack.awk>>=
#!/usr/bin/gawk -f
BEGIN { ORS = " " }
/@xref chunkbegin / {
    valid += 1
    print $3
}
/@xref chunkuse / {
    if (valid != 1)
    {
        print "Invalid @xref chunks." > "/dev/stderr"
        exit 1
    }
    print substr($0, 16)# rule pattern has length 15
}
/@xref chunkend/ { valid = 0; printf "\n" }
@

The following is a translation of that program that instead works on the
parse tree of the tool syntax. Unlike the AWK code it is inelegant.
<<set [[g-nodes]] and [[g-edges]]>>=
;; TODO: fetch a parse tree from peg-noweb; set `g-nodes' and `g-edges'
(let ((tree (peg-noweb-parse-files (whyse-project-script project)))
      (document (peg-noweb-document 1 tree))
      (indices (peg-noweb-document-indices document)))
  (cl-first indices))
@

