\section{Emacs LISP autorecognize definitions}
\label{sec:emacs-lisp-definitions}

The built-in package \texttt{PEG} greatly simplifies the source code of this
package: rather than relying on an autodefs.elisp file written in AWK we can use
PEG parsing to manage Emacs LISP definitions when we see them. The following
PEXs are based off the AWK code written by Joseph S. Riel.

The following grammar should be parameterizable using an Emacs LISP
customization varible which allows users to list the symbols of functions and
macros which define things. For example, \texttt{define-peg-rule} and
\texttt{define-peg-ruleset} define PEG-related things, but when peg-noweb is
parsing a noweb with emacs lisp which defines peg rules and rulesets the
identifiers defined with these functions or macros would not be marked up
properly. Therefore, at least within Emacs, we can use Emacs LISP to mark up
Noweb texts with the necessary @defn options PEG parsing rather than regular
expressions in AWK.

<<simple lisp grammar>>=
(define-peg-rule lisp-atom ()
  (substring (+ (and (not (or [blank "()"] "\n")) (any))))
  (* (or [blank] "\n")))
(define-peg-rule lisp-form ()
  "(" (opt [blank]) (list (or "nil" lisp-atom) (* (or lisp-atom lisp-form))) ")")
@ 

Recognizing a LISP form at the ``top level'' of web text will allow parsing;
attempting to recognize forms at lower levels will lead to the misidentification
of forms that are nested within others that may be quoted, for instance, in
strings or other forms that quote others.

For example, only one top-level definition is given below.

<<example-n>>=
(defvar example-defun-form
  (quote (defun example () "Hello, world!")))
(eval example-defun-form)
(example)
@ 

The top-level definition is useful for higher-order programming, evaluating data
as code, which is normal in LISP, but at the top-level of the program text
itself the function ``example'' was not defined until run time, so recognizing
the identifier may be misguided in larger, more complex programs. Therefore the
recognizer is limited to top level forms.

The grammar defined above for LISP forms is limited to the recognition of raw
text and does not parse LISP forms found spread across multiple ``@text''
keywords in Noweb tool syntax.

<<define functions>>=
;; Derived from AWK code released under the GNU GPL v2 license by Joseph S.
;; Riel; that code is Copyright (c) 2005-2015 by Joseph S. Riel. All rights
;; reserved.
(defun peg-noweb--emacs-lisp-identifier-recognizer ()
  "This function must only be called when the parser is within a code
chunk, at the beginning of the line of a @text keyword."
  (with-peg-rules
      ((whitespace (+ (or " " "\t")))
       (identifier (substring (+ (not (or " " "\t" "\n" "()"))))
                   `(id -- (cons 'index id) id))
       (definitions (bol) "@text" whitespace
                    "(" (opt whitespace)
                    ;; TODO: for performance, the following form should be cached!
                    (or (funcall #'mapcar #'symbol-name peg-noweb-define-symbols))

                    whitespace identifier (opt whitespace)))
    (peg-run (peg definitions))))

<<define customization variables>>=
(defcustom peg-noweb-define-symbols
  (list 'defun
        'defvar
        'defconst
        'defgroup
        'defalias
        'defsubst
        'defmacro
        'defcustom
        'defadvice
        'defimage
        'define-minor-mode
        'define-derived-mode
        'define-abbrev
        'define-category
        'define-prefix-command
        'define-key-after)
  "The symbols in Emacs LISP which define things like variables, functions, macros, modes, etc.

Symbols do not need to be `intern'-ed before parsing with PEG noweb, as only the `symbol-name' is used."
  :type '(repeat symbol)
  :group 'peg-noweb)

@










